// Waiting for a time to pass, without holding an operating-system thread.
//
// API
// ---
//   timer.sleep_ms(ms)    -> void, returns once `ms` milliseconds have passed
//
// **`sleep_ms` parks the calling green thread and nothing else.** The
// carrier OS thread it was running on goes on running other green threads for
// the whole wait, so a thousand sleepers on two carriers finish in about one
// interval, not five hundred of them. The deadline is kept by the reactor
// thread, which is also what bounds a socket read
// (docs/net-timeouts-decision.md); this is the same wait with no descriptor
// to be woken by.
//
// It sleeps for **at least** `ms` milliseconds on the monotonic clock (the
// reactor rounds its own wake-up up, never down), and for as much longer as it
// takes the scheduler to give the thread a turn back. `sleep_ms(0)` returns at
// once without parking, and does not yield.
//
// A negative `ms` is a bug in the caller, as it is for `set_read_timeout`,
// and traps: there is no "for ever" here, because a thread that sleeps for
// ever on nothing can only be a mistake. Waiting for ever for something is
// what a channel `recv` is for.
//
// What is deliberately not here: a monotonic clock to read (`date.now_ns` is
// the wall clock, and says so), a cancellable sleep (a thread that has to
// stop waiting early waits on a channel with a sender instead), and
// sub-millisecond resolution (a `prim` carries milliseconds, like every other
// timeout in the standard library).

// Park the green thread for `timeout_ms` with no descriptor to wake it
// earlier: the same primitive `net` bounds its waits with, called with fd -1
// and no events. 1 when the time has passed, -errno for a refused argument.
prim int __wait_io_timeout(int fd, int events, int timeout_ms);

/// Return after `ms` milliseconds, parking only this green thread.
///
/// Traps on a negative `ms`.
pub void sleep_ms(int ms) {
    if (ms < 0) {
        trap("timer.sleep_ms(" + ms.to_str() + "): a duration cannot be negative");
    }
    int n = __wait_io_timeout(-1, 0, ms);
    if (n < 0) {
        trap("timer.sleep_ms(" + ms.to_str() + "): the runtime refused the wait (errno " + (-n).to_str() + ")");
    }
}
